ative coordinates I established for the reference. I'm ensuring the 2's position aligns with the flip transformation, testing the horizontal flip hypothesis. I'm verifying 2's placement within the coordinate system.\n\n\n**Testing Rotations Further**\n\nI'm now investigating potential rotations of the reference shape. Initial 180-degree rotation didn't fully align. Now I'm carefully mapping the positions of the '4's to see if I can derive the exact transformation applied to the reference to match shape 1. The key is in matching the relative positions and checking the dot positions as well. I'm considering all possibilities.\n\n\n\n**Determining Square Symmetries**\n\nI've made significant progress. I've realized the reference shape is symmetric under a transpose operation, which simplifies symmetry analysis. I've identified the 90-degree clockwise rotation as the transformation mapping the reference shape to Shape 1, and the dot position, especially the location of the 2, matches perfectly. Now, I'm verifying this rotation using the other shapes.\n\n\n\n**Confirming Shape Transformations**\n\nI've just confirmed Shape 2 is a transpose of the reference, as expected. I'm verifying the transformed dot positions against the transpose mapping, and it's holding up perfectly! I'm now moving on to Shape 3. I've analyzed its structure of 4s, and found an invariance. I now need to determine the transformation mapping the 2, which I think is a horizontal flip.\n\n\n**Calculating Dot Outputs**\n\nI've determined that Shape 3 is indeed a horizontal flip. I'm now calculating the output dot placements for all shapes by applying their respective transformations (90-degree clockwise rotation for Shape 1, transpose for Shape 2, and horizontal flip for Shape 3) to the reference shape's dot coordinates. I'm using relative coordinates to map the reference dots to the correct grid positions for each shape. I'm cross-verifying each output.\n\n\n\n",
